//! `sos` — the SOS userspace API (design 140; sos/spec.md §2, §5.7, §12).
//!
//! U-MODE LIBRARY CODE, OWNED AND EXPORTED BY THE KERNEL PACKAGE. Nothing here
//! runs in kernel mode; all of it lives in the kernel's tree. That is the
//! Zircon-vDSO discipline (ratified Aug 5) and it buys one specific thing:
//! SYSCALL NUMBERS ARE NOT ABI. The op numbers come from `sosabi`, which the
//! kernel's dispatch tables import too, so the two halves of the contract
//! cannot skew — and because a process links this module instead of writing
//! numbers, the kernel may renumber between builds without breaking anything
//! that was compiled against it.
//!
//! THREE ENTRY ALTITUDES, ONE IMPLEMENTATION CHAIN. There is exactly one place
//! in the system that issues a trap, and everything above it is a wrapper:
//!
//!   typed Saw   `system.shutdown(0)`                 <- prefer this
//!   typed C     `sos_system_shutdown(h, 0)`          <- for non-Saw languages
//!   raw         `sos_syscall1(h, op, arg)`           <- the HAL stub
//!
//! The Saw methods below call the `@export`ed C functions, which call the raw
//! form, which is the per-arch `ecall`/`svc` stub in `sos/hal/<arch>/user/`.
//! No altitude reimplements the one below it, so there is no second trap path
//! to keep in step.
//!
//! WHAT A PROCESS MUST STILL AGREE WITH THE KERNEL ABOUT: the calling
//! convention (§5.7 — handle in a0, op in a7, status back in a0) and the status
//! tags it branches on. The first lives in the HAL stub; the second is
//! `SosStatus` below. Neither is a number this file's callers ever write.
//!
//! THE TWO LAYERS, AND WHICH ONE CARRIES A GUARANTEE (ruled Aug 15, user). The
//! TYPED SAW SURFACE NEVER EXPOSES OR ACCEPTS A RAW HANDLE WORD: `System`,
//! `Process` and `Thread` keep private fields, offer no word extractor and no
//! word constructor, and every one of them is minted INSIDE this module by the
//! op wrapper that received it from the kernel — so the only crossing INTO the
//! typed layer is `System(boot_handle:)`, §12's root of trust, and there is no
//! crossing out at all. A handle you hold is one the kernel handed you, and a
//! handle you were never given is not a thing you can spell.
//! The `@export`ed C SURFACE below is the FLOOR and keeps raw `UInt` handles,
//! because a C caller has no type system to carry the guarantee in. That is a
//! documented difference in kind, not an oversight: the C altitude buys a
//! non-Saw process the same operations with none of the same protection, and a
//! process that wants the protection writes Saw.
//!
//! WHICH ENUMS ARE PUBLIC API, AND WHICH ARE BOOKKEEPING (ruled Aug 16, user).
//! The vDSO discipline is about OP NUMBERS, not about argument encodings, and
//! the line runs exactly there:
//!
//!   PUBLIC — an enum or record whose values a PROCESS CHOOSES and passes as a
//!   syscall argument, or that the kernel HANDS BACK as data. `EventMode` is
//!   the first kind: a caller names it (`event_create(mode:
//!   EventMode.SaturatingSum)`), so it is API, and the alternative — a method
//!   per mode — reads fine at two and stops scaling at the next object, where
//!   M3's Mapping brings an access mode AND a memory type and a method per
//!   combination is a matrix rather than a list. `WaitTag` / `WaitPayload` /
//!   `WaitResult` and the wait record's word offsets are the second kind: the
//!   kernel WRITES that record and userspace READS it, so its layout is a
//!   contract between the two and one declaration has to serve both. Mapping
//!   inherits both patterns.
//!
//!   KERNEL-INTERNAL — op numbers, rights bits, `ObjType`, `FaultReason`, the
//!   dispatch's bookkeeping. A process never writes one of these, which is what
//!   leaves the kernel free to renumber between builds.
//!
//! EXACTLY ONE DECLARATION EITHER WAY, and for a public one that declaration
//! stays in `sosabi` — the kernel's dispatch decodes the same enum, and a second
//! copy here is precisely the skew that file exists to prevent. Saw's imports
//! RE-EXPORT, so the one declaration is reachable through this module and a
//! process writes `import sos.{EventMode}`. It is worth knowing that the
//! re-export is INDISCRIMINATE — everything imported here is reachable through
//! here, and `sosabi` is on a process's module path as a transitive dependency
//! regardless — so the split above is held by what userspace is TOLD to write
//! rather than by a wall. That was already true; the rule only says which side
//! of the line each family sits on.
//!
//! (The other direction was probed and does not work: the enum cannot MOVE here
//! and be imported by the kernel, because this module and the kernel HAL export
//! the two runtime hooks under the same C names for their two sides — importing
//! one into the other is a duplicate-`@export` error, since a Saw import
//! compiles the whole module into the unit.)

// THE ENCODINGS A PROCESS IS MEANT TO WRITE OR MATCH ON, and the only things
// this module re-exports (design 229): the status tag every `Result<_,
// SosStatus>` hands back, the mode a `event_create(mode:)` caller names, and
// the record + payload a `wait()` caller matches on. `import sos.{SosStatus}`
// (etc.) is how a process takes them.
public import sosabi.{SosStatus, EventMode, ClockType, WaitResult, WaitPayload}

// Everything else `sosabi` holds is KERNEL-INTERNAL and stays that way: the op
// numbers, the rights, the handle aliases, the wait record's wire layout. This
// is an ordinary import, so a process cannot reach one through `sos` under any
// spelling — the vDSO discipline ("syscall numbers are not ABI") is a wall now
// rather than a convention the module docstring asks callers to respect.
import sosabi.{SystemOp, ProcessOp, ThreadOp, EventOp, WaiterOp, InterruptOp,
               ClockOp, TimerOp,
               SystemHandle, ProcessHandle, ThreadHandle, EventHandle,
               WaiterHandle, InterruptHandle, ClockHandle, TimerHandle,
               WaitTag, WAIT_RECORD_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD, WAIT_TAG_WORD,
               WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD, wait_record_bytes,
               CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS, CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT,
               clock_now_record_bytes,
               TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS, TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT,
               TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT, timer_arm_record_bytes}

// The scalar-to-console-byte rule, shared with the kernel's own console so the
// two cannot disagree about what a non-ASCII scalar becomes.
import sosrt.{ascii_byte}

// The per-arch HAL, and since design 172 part 2 the WHOLE of it: the one
// instruction in the system that crosses the trap boundary. FIXED ARITY on
// purpose — a varargs call across a trap boundary has no portable register
// story, so a wider op gets `sos_syscall2` beside this, not an ellipsis.
extern "C" {
    func sos_syscall1(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt) -> UInt
    func sos_syscall3(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt, arg2: UInt,
                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) -> UInt
}


// =============================================================================
// Errors
// =============================================================================

// How a syscall reports what happened is `sosabi`'s `SosStatus` (spec §5.7) —
// the SAME declaration the kernel's dispatch returns, so the two halves of the
// contract cannot disagree about what tag 3 means. It carries its own
// `describe()`, `Printable` and `Error` (design 145: an enum takes methods and
// hand-written trait bodies), which is what retired the free `sys_error(status)`
// helper this module used to need. A process reaches all of it through the
// value it is handed and never imports `sosabi` — the type's own module is
// always consulted for its methods (design 142), and an enum case pattern
// resolves from the scrutinee.
//
// So userspace can now interpolate a failure (`print("{}", e)`) as well as
// `match` it. Before, the type could conform to nothing and had to be branched
// on to say anything at all.
//
// IT IS A STATUS, AND ONLY SOME STATUSES ARE ERRORS. `checked` below is the one
// place that distinction is made, and it is what keeps the `Ok` case out of the
// error channel entirely.

/// Turn a raw status word into a Result. The one place a number becomes a type.
///
/// THE SPLIT (ratified Aug 7): `Ok` becomes `Result.Ok`, everything else becomes
/// `Err(status)`. So the `Ok` case exists on the wire, where 0 has to mean
/// something, and cannot exist in an `Err` — `Err(SosStatus.Ok)`, a value that
/// would claim a call both failed and succeeded, is unconstructible downstream
/// because this function is the only constructor of the error channel.
///
/// The narrowing is guarded rather than assumed: `a0` is register-wide, the enum
/// is byte-wide (its width is ABI), and an `as UInt8` on a value above 255 is a
/// PANIC — in a process, over a word the process did not choose. A kernel
/// compiled from this same table never returns one, so the guard is unreachable
/// in M1 and costs a compare; what it buys is that the impossible case is an
/// error value rather than a trap.
func checked(raw: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    if raw > (UInt8.max as UInt) {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    guard let status = SosStatus.from(raw: raw as UInt8) else {
        return SosStatus.Unknown
    }
    if status.is_failure() {
        return status
    }
    return
}

/// The same split for an op that answers with a VALUE — a created thread's
/// handle, a joined thread's exit code, a status word. `Ok` carries the value;
/// anything else is the error and the value is not read.
func checked_value(raw: UInt, value: UInt) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    try checked(raw)
    return value
}

/// Issue an op that answers with a value.
///
/// `unsafe` because it names a pointer: the value register comes back through
/// an out-parameter, which is the shape that crosses a C ABI with no aggregate
/// return. The typed methods below call this and stay safe signatures — the
/// pointer never leaves this function, which is design 136's confinement rule.
func call_value(handle: UInt, op: UInt, arg0: UInt, arg1: UInt,
                arg2: UInt) unsafe -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_syscall3(handle, op, arg0, arg1, arg2,
                           (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    checked_value(raw, value)
}


// =============================================================================
// The C-ABI surface (spec §5.7) — for non-Saw languages
// =============================================================================
//
// PER-OP functions are the supported C interface: one function per operation,
// named for it, with the arguments the operation actually takes. A C caller
// gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the type wrapper, and still
// never writes an op number.
//
// `sos_syscall1` underneath is the raw form and is reachable, but it is the
// escape hatch, not the interface: it takes an op number, which is the thing
// this whole arrangement exists to keep out of callers.
//
// Whitelist-clean by construction — handles, ops and statuses are all
// integers, so nothing here needs a type the C ABI cannot carry.

/// Write one byte to the debug console. Requires `SystemRight.Debug`.
/// Returns a status word: 0 on success.
@export("sos_system_debug_print")
public func sos_system_debug_print(handle: UInt, byte: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.DebugPrint as UInt, byte)
}

/// Stop the machine with `status`. Requires `SystemRight.Shutdown`. Does not
/// return on success; returns a status word if the call was refused.
@export("sos_system_shutdown")
public func sos_system_shutdown(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, SystemOp.Shutdown as UInt, status)
}

// The ops that answer with a VALUE take an out-parameter for it, exactly as the
// raw form does. A C caller writes `unsigned long h; sos_process_thread_self(p,
// &h);` and gets the same contract a Saw caller does minus the Result.

/// This process's own handle. Requires `SystemRight.Manage`.
@export("sos_system_process_self")
public func sos_system_process_self(handle: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ProcessSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a thread in this process, ready to start. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`. `entry` may be `ENTRY_IMAGE`.
@export("sos_process_thread_create")
public func sos_process_thread_create(handle: UInt, entry: UInt, stack_top: UInt,
                                      arg: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadCreate as UInt, entry, stack_top, arg,
                 value_out)
}

/// The CALLING thread's own handle. Requires `ProcessRight.Manage`.
@export("sos_process_thread_self")
public func sos_process_thread_self(handle: UInt,
                                    value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.ThreadSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this process with `status`. Requires `ProcessRight.Exit`. Does not
/// return on success.
@export("sos_process_exit")
public func sos_process_exit(handle: UInt, status: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ProcessOp.Exit as UInt, status)
}

/// This process's §8 status word. Requires `ProcessRight.Wait`.
@export("sos_process_get_status")
public func sos_process_get_status(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.GetStatus as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a created thread runnable. Requires `ThreadRight.Start`.
@export("sos_thread_start")
public func sos_thread_start(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Start as UInt, 0)
}

/// Wait for a thread and take its exit value. Requires `ThreadRight.Join`.
@export("sos_thread_join")
public func sos_thread_join(handle: UInt,
                            value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ThreadOp.Join as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End the CALLING thread with `code`. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the
/// caller's own handle. Does not return.
@export("sos_thread_exit")
public func sos_thread_exit(handle: UInt, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Exit as UInt, code)
}

/// Give the processor up. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the caller's own
/// handle.
@export("sos_thread_yield")
public func sos_thread_yield(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, ThreadOp.Yield as UInt, 0)
}

/// Make an Event in this process. Requires `ProcessRight.EventCreate`. `mode`
/// is 0 for bitwise-OR accumulation and 1 for saturating-sum (spec §2.4); any
/// other value terminates the process.
///
/// THE C ALTITUDE WRITES THE MODE AS A NUMBER, and that is the documented
/// difference in kind the module docstring describes rather than an oversight:
/// a C caller has no enum to be handed, so it gets the wire value. The Saw
/// altitude above never writes one — it offers a method per mode.
@export("sos_process_event_create")
public func sos_process_event_create(handle: UInt, mode: UInt,
                                     value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.EventCreate as UInt, mode, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Make a Waiter in this process. Requires `ProcessRight.WaiterCreate`.
@export("sos_process_waiter_create")
public func sos_process_waiter_create(handle: UInt,
                                      value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.WaiterCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Bind IRQ `line` to a new Interrupt in this process. Requires
/// `ProcessRight.InterruptBind`. A line this machine does not have, the
/// timer's line, or one already bound TERMINATES the process.
@export("sos_process_interrupt_bind")
public func sos_process_interrupt_bind(handle: UInt, line: UInt,
                                         value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ProcessOp.InterruptBind as UInt, line, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// End this fire and let the device interrupt again. Requires
/// `InterruptRight.Ack`. Acking one that has not fired terminates the process.
@export("sos_interrupt_ack")
public func sos_interrupt_ack(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, InterruptOp.Ack as UInt, 0)
}

/// Accumulate `bits` into an Event. Never blocks. Requires `EventRight.Signal`.
@export("sos_event_signal")
public func sos_event_signal(handle: UInt, bits: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, EventOp.Signal as UInt, bits)
}

/// Drain an Event's word and reset it to zero. Requires `EventRight.Receive`.
@export("sos_event_receive")
public func sos_event_receive(handle: UInt,
                              value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, EventOp.Receive as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Attach a waitable to a Waiter under `key`, which must not already be in use
/// on that Waiter. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` on the waiter and
/// `EventRight.Wait` on the target.
///
/// It is the first op taking TWO arguments and answering with a status alone,
/// and the raw forms are fixed-arity by design (§5.7: no varargs across a trap
/// boundary), so it rides the three-argument form and drops the value. The
/// scratch that catches it is what makes this signature `unsafe`; the pointer
/// never leaves the function, which is design 136's confinement rule.
@export("sos_waiter_add")
public func sos_waiter_add(handle: UInt, target: UInt, key: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Add as UInt, target, key, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Detach the attachment registered under `key`. Requires
/// `WaiterRight.Attach`.
///
/// IT TAKES THE KEY, NOT THE WAITABLE'S HANDLE: detaching edits the Waiter's
/// own table and never touches the waitable, so the key is the whole of what
/// names the attachment.
@export("sos_waiter_remove")
public func sos_waiter_remove(handle: UInt, key: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, WaiterOp.Remove as UInt, key)
}

/// Block until an attached handle is ready, and COPY OUT a record describing it
/// into `buf` (spec §2.2, as amended by design 178 unit 3 rider 3). Requires
/// `WaiterRight.Wait`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `wait_record_bytes()`. The buffer must be word-aligned and inside this
/// process's writable memory; a buffer that is not TERMINATES the process,
/// because it is a mistake only the caller could have made.
///
/// The record's layout is public: word 0 the attacher's key, word 1 a `WaitTag`,
/// word 2 the payload. A C caller reads the words; a Saw caller gets them
/// decoded into a `WaitResult` by `Waiter.wait` below.
@export("sos_waiter_wait")
public func sos_waiter_wait(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                            cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, WaiterOp.Wait as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// This process's Clock for the domain `kind` names. Requires
/// `SystemRight.ClockGet`. A `kind` that is not a `ClockType` terminates the
/// process.
///
/// The same handle every time — a machine has one monotonic clock, so asking
/// twice is a query rather than two capabilities.
@export("sos_system_clock_get")
public func sos_system_clock_get(handle: UInt, kind: UInt,
                                 value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, SystemOp.ClockGet as UInt, kind, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Read the clock into `buf` — a COPY-OUT record, because a nanosecond count is
/// 64 bits and one profile's registers are not. Requires `ClockRight.Read`.
///
/// `cap` is the buffer's size in BYTES and must be at least
/// `clock_now_record_bytes()`. Doubleword 0 is the reading.
@export("sos_clock_now")
public func sos_clock_now(handle: UInt, buf: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          cap: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.Now as UInt, buf as UInt, cap, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Make a Timer bound to this Clock. Requires `ClockRight.TimerCreate`.
@export("sos_clock_timer_create")
public func sos_clock_timer_create(handle: UInt,
                                   value_out: UnsafePointer<UInt>) unsafe -> UInt {
    sos_syscall3(handle, ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0, value_out)
}

/// Arm a Timer from a COPY-IN record at `record` — doubleword 0 the delay from
/// now, doubleword 1 the repeat interval (0 for one-shot), both in nanoseconds.
/// Requires `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// THIS IS THE COPY-OUT FUNNEL'S MIRROR TWIN, and the first op in the system to
/// hand the kernel a record rather than take one. `bytes` must be EXACTLY
/// `timer_arm_record_bytes()`; a record that is misaligned, outside this
/// process's memory, or the wrong length TERMINATES the process, because all
/// three are mistakes only the caller could have made.
///
/// Arming an already-armed Timer REPLACES its schedule and clears any fires it
/// had not yet delivered. That is not an error — it is how a timeout is
/// changed.
@export("sos_timer_arm")
public func sos_timer_arm(handle: UInt, record: UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          bytes: UInt) unsafe -> UInt {
    var ignored: UInt = 0
    sos_syscall3(handle, TimerOp.Arm as UInt, record as UInt, bytes, 0,
                 (&var ignored) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
}

/// Stop a Timer and discard any fires it had not delivered. Requires
/// `TimerRight.Arm`.
///
/// Disarming one that is not armed is a NO-OP, deliberately — a one-shot
/// disarms itself when it fires, so cancelling a timeout that has just expired
/// is an ordinary race rather than a caller error. (This is the opposite call
/// from `sos_interrupt_ack`, whose no-fire case IS a fault; see `Timer.disarm`.)
@export("sos_timer_disarm")
public func sos_timer_disarm(handle: UInt) -> UInt {
    sos_syscall1(handle, TimerOp.Disarm as UInt, 0)
}

// =============================================================================
// The typed Saw surface
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to the kernel's System object — in M1 the whole of a process's
/// authority (§12's boot handle set, one handle wide today).
///
/// The field is a `SystemHandle`, not a `UInt`: one kind of handle cannot be
/// built from a raw word or from another kind's handle, so handle-kind
/// confusion is not expressible here even before the ops are considered. It
/// still costs nothing — the alias flows to `UInt` implicitly at the stub, so
/// the calls below pass `self.handle` with no cast and no conversion is emitted.
///
/// Real handles will be `NoCopy` with a `Deinit` that closes them (§4); this
/// one is a plain value because M1 has no close op and root holds its handle
/// for the life of the machine. That is the second tier and it is additive:
/// the `NoCopy` wrapper takes this alias as its payload.
public struct System {
    handle: SystemHandle,
}

public extension System {
    /// Adopt the boot handle the kernel passed in the first argument register.
    ///
    /// The parameter is a raw `UInt` because that is what a register holds and
    /// what the ABI specifies; this is one of the two places in the system that
    /// crosses INTO a handle type (the other is the kernel's dispatch, after
    /// the table has resolved it). Crossing at CREATION is what the explicit
    /// construction is for.
    public init(boot_handle: UInt) -> System {
        // The runtime's panic path prints through this same handle, so it has
        // to be parked where the seams below can reach it before any Saw code
        // can fail.
        park_system_handle(boot_handle)
        System(handle: SystemHandle(boot_handle))
    }

    /// Write one byte to the debug console.
    public func debug_print_byte(&self, byte: UInt8) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_system_debug_print(self.handle, byte as UInt))
    }

    /// Write an ASCII string. Stops at the FIRST failure and reports it — a
    /// partial write is surfaced, never swallowed.
    public func debug_print(&self, msg: String) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        for scalar in msg.chars() {
            try self.debug_print_byte(ascii_byte(scalar))
        }
        return
    }

    /// Stop the machine with `status`. Does not return when it succeeds; a
    /// refusal (no `SystemRight.Shutdown`, say) comes back as an error.
    public func shutdown(&self, status: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_system_shutdown(self.handle, status))
    }

    /// This process's own Process object (§3's derivation rule: a handle is
    /// obtained through a handle already held). Requires `SystemRight.Manage`.
    ///
    /// The same handle every time — the process's table has ONE entry naming
    /// it, and §3 forbids duplicating a handle — so calling this twice is a
    /// query, not two capabilities.
    public func process_self(&self) -> Result<Process, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, SystemOp.ProcessSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
        return Process(handle: ProcessHandle(raw))
    }

    /// This process's Clock for `kind` — a GRANTED TIME SOURCE. Requires
    /// `SystemRight.ClockGet`.
    ///
    /// TIME IS A CAPABILITY HERE, and that is the whole reason this is an op
    /// rather than a free function. A process that was not granted the right
    /// cannot read the machine's clock at all — which is what makes handing a
    /// child a VIRTUAL clock later a substitution with no code change on either
    /// side: the child asks its System object for a Clock exactly as it does
    /// now, and what it gets back is somebody else's idea of the time.
    ///
    /// The same Clock every time — a machine has one monotonic clock, and §3
    /// forbids duplicating a handle — so calling this twice is a query, not two
    /// capabilities. `ClockType.Monotonic` is the only domain v1 declares; it
    /// counts nanoseconds from an unpublished epoch, never steps and never goes
    /// backwards, so two readings are comparable and one means nothing alone.
    ///
    /// THE LABEL IS `kind:` AND THE RULED SPELLING WAS `type:` — see DF-232b.
    /// `type` is a Saw keyword (it introduces a distinct type alias), so it
    /// cannot be a parameter name; `kind` is the vocabulary the rest of this
    /// system already uses for the same idea (`ProcessStatusKind`,
    /// `WaitableKind`).
    public func clock_get(&self,
                          kind: ClockType = ClockType.Monotonic) -> Result<Clock, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, SystemOp.ClockGet as UInt,
                                 kind as UInt, 0, 0)
        return Clock(handle: ClockHandle(raw))
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Process (spec §2, §7, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to a Process — an address-space domain, a handle table and threads.
///
/// A process reaches its OWN through `System.process_self`. Holding somebody
/// else's is what supervision will look like when there is somebody else (M3).
///
/// THERE IS NO WAY TO BUILD ONE FROM A WORD and no way to get a word back out.
/// The field is private (design 80), the only constructors are this module's own
/// op wrappers, and the derivation chain starts at `System(boot_handle:)` — so
/// a `Process` in hand is one the kernel handed over, not an integer somebody
/// guessed. See the module docstring's two-layer rule.
public struct Process {
    handle: ProcessHandle,
}

public extension Process {
    /// Make a thread at `entry`, ready to be started. Requires
    /// `ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`.
    ///
    /// The thread runs with `stack_top` as its stack pointer and `arg` in its
    /// first argument register, and it must NOT return: its link register is
    /// zero, so returning faults and the fault ends the process (spec §8).
    ///
    /// An entry or a stack outside this process TERMINATES it (the faults
    /// ruling — a process knows where its own memory is). A full thread slab or
    /// handle table comes back as `SosStatus.NoResource`, which it could not
    /// have known.
    public func thread_create(&self, entry: UInt, stack_top: UInt,
                              arg: UInt) -> Result<Thread, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.ThreadCreate as UInt,
                                 entry, stack_top, arg)
        return Thread(handle: ThreadHandle(raw))
    }

    /// Make a thread that starts at this process's own IMAGE ENTRY — the entry
    /// the kernel took out of the image header — with `arg` in its first
    /// argument register, so the entry can tell which thread it is running as.
    ///
    /// THIS OVERLOAD EXISTS BECAUSE OF A LANGUAGE GAP, and it is worth knowing
    /// which: Saw cannot name the address of a function (design 172's reason 3,
    /// DF-172a — `extern` declares functions, an extern function is not usable
    /// as a value, and `@export` on a static emits a definition). So a process
    /// written entirely in Saw cannot compute an entry address at all, and this
    /// is the one entry it can ask for by name. A process with a C leaf that
    /// CAN produce an address uses the overload above. See DF-178c.
    public func thread_create(&self, stack_top: UInt,
                              arg: UInt) -> Result<Thread, SosStatus> {
        self.thread_create(entry: sosabi.ENTRY_IMAGE, stack_top: stack_top,
                           arg: arg)
    }

    /// The CALLING thread's own Thread object. Requires `ProcessRight.Manage`.
    ///
    /// This is how a thread reaches the handle it needs to exit or yield: it is
    /// handed its process, and asks the process which thread it is.
    public func thread_self(&self) -> Result<Thread, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.ThreadSelf as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
        return Thread(handle: ThreadHandle(raw))
    }

    /// End this process with `status`. Every thread goes with it and the kernel
    /// closes every handle it held (spec §2's Process row). Does not return
    /// when it succeeds. Requires `ProcessRight.Exit`.
    public func exit(&self, status: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_process_exit(self.handle, status))
    }

    /// This process's §8 status word: kind in the high bits, code in the low
    /// ones. Requires `ProcessRight.Wait`.
    public func get_status(&self) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
        call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.GetStatus as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
    }

    /// Make an Event accumulating in `mode` — §2.4's per-Event property, fixed
    /// for the object's life. Requires `ProcessRight.EventCreate`.
    ///
    /// `EventMode.Or` is the flag set, "which of these events occurred since
    /// last drain", and it is the default because it is the signal use case.
    /// `EventMode.SaturatingSum` is the counting semaphore, "how many times",
    /// and it saturates rather than wrapping — so a producer faster than its
    /// consumer loses count but never rolls back through zero, which is the
    /// value that means "not ready".
    ///
    /// ONE METHOD TAKING THE MODE, not one method per mode (ruled Aug 16,
    /// user, reversing this module's first answer). A method per mode reads
    /// fine at two and stops scaling immediately: M3's Mapping brings an access
    /// mode and a memory type, and a method per combination is a matrix rather
    /// than a list. The mode is a VALUE the caller chooses, so it is spelled
    /// as one. See the module docstring for which enums are public API.
    public func event_create(&self,
                             mode: EventMode = EventMode.Or) -> Result<Event, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.EventCreate as UInt,
                                 mode as UInt, 0, 0)
        return Event(handle: EventHandle(raw))
    }

    /// Make a Waiter — §2.2's generic wait aggregator. Requires
    /// `ProcessRight.WaiterCreate`.
    public func waiter_create(&self) -> Result<Waiter, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.WaiterCreate as UInt,
                                 0, 0, 0)
        return Waiter(handle: WaiterHandle(raw))
    }

    /// Bind IRQ `line` to an Interrupt — spec §2's row and §9's delivery.
    /// Requires `ProcessRight.InterruptBind`.
    ///
    /// THE LINE IS A NUMBER THE CALLER CHOOSES, so it is spelled as one, which
    /// is the same rule `event_create`'s mode follows. A driver knows its
    /// device's line from the device's own description; the kernel checks it
    /// against the board and TERMINATES the process for a line this machine
    /// does not have, the timer's line, or one already bound — all three
    /// caller-checkable, and all three the faults ruling's kind of mistake.
    ///
    /// The line comes back UNMASKED, which is §9's starting state: the object
    /// exists, the controller will deliver, and the first fire masks it until
    /// an `ack`. What the kernel does NOT touch is the device's own
    /// interrupt-enable register — that is behind the driver's granted window
    /// and is the driver's to write.
    public func interrupt_bind(&self, line: UInt) -> Result<Interrupt, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ProcessOp.InterruptBind as UInt,
                                 line, 0, 0)
        return Interrupt(handle: InterruptHandle(raw))
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Thread (spec §2, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to a Thread — a kernel-scheduled execution context in a process.
///
/// Saw's own TaskGroups run INSIDE one of these, in userspace; the kernel never
/// sees a task (spec §2).
///
/// Like `Process`, it cannot be built from a word or turned back into one: a
/// `Thread` comes from `Process.thread_create` or `Process.thread_self` and from
/// nowhere else. Naming a thread you were never given is therefore not
/// expressible in this layer — which is the point, and which is why the harness
/// case that PROVES the kernel rejects such a handle is a hand-written payload
/// at the raw altitude rather than a program written against this module.
public struct Thread {
    handle: ThreadHandle,
}

public extension Thread {
    /// Make a created thread runnable. It runs when the scheduler next picks
    /// it, which is not necessarily before this call returns. Requires
    /// `ThreadRight.Start`.
    public func start(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_thread_start(self.handle))
    }

    /// Wait for this thread to exit and take its exit value. Requires
    /// `ThreadRight.Join`.
    ///
    /// An already-exited thread answers immediately; a running one parks the
    /// caller until it exits. Joining YOURSELF terminates the process — it is a
    /// wait that could never end, and the caller could have checked.
    public func join(&self) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
        call_value(self.handle, ThreadOp.Join as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
    }

    /// End the CALLING thread with `code`, which whoever joins it receives.
    /// Does not return. Requires `ThreadRight.Control`, and the handle must
    /// name the calling thread.
    public func exit(&self, code: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_thread_exit(self.handle, code))
    }

    /// Give the processor up to the next runnable thread, and go to the back of
    /// the queue. Requires `ThreadRight.Control` on the caller's own handle.
    ///
    /// Named for `std.task`'s `yield_now` so that the kernel-thread spelling
    /// and the cooperative-task spelling read alike — they are the same idea at
    /// two scales, and a reader should not have to learn two words.
    public func yield_now(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_thread_yield(self.handle))
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Event (spec §2.4; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to an Event — the accumulating non-blocking notification.
///
/// Bits go in through `signal` and never block; one `receive` takes them all
/// out and resets the word. A non-zero word is what "ready" means to a Waiter,
/// which is the only other thing that ever looks at it.
///
/// Built by `Process.event_create` and by nothing else — no word constructor,
/// no word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
public struct Event {
    handle: EventHandle,
}

public extension Event {
    /// Accumulate `bits`. NEVER BLOCKS and never fails for want of room: under
    /// OR the word saturates by being idempotent, and under counting it
    /// saturates by refusing to wrap. Requires `EventRight.Signal`.
    ///
    /// Signalling ZERO is a no-op that leaves the event exactly as ready as it
    /// already was — it cannot make one ready, because zero IS "not ready".
    public func signal(&self, bits: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_event_signal(self.handle, bits))
    }

    /// Drain the accumulated word and reset it to zero, in one operation.
    /// Answers zero for an event nothing has signalled — it does not park, which
    /// is the Waiter's job. Requires `EventRight.Receive`.
    public func receive(&self) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
        call_value(self.handle, EventOp.Receive as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Interrupt (spec §2 table, §9; design 178 D6)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to an Interrupt — an IRQ line bound to a waitable.
///
/// THE WHOLE OF A USERSPACE DRIVER'S KERNEL SURFACE, and it is two operations
/// wide: attach it to a Waiter and park, then `ack` when the device has been
/// serviced. Everything between those — reading the device's registers,
/// deciding what the interrupt meant, writing a reply — happens in ordinary
/// process code through the MMIO window the image was granted. There is no
/// kernel driver, and this type is the reason there does not have to be one.
///
/// SPEC §9'S CYCLE, from the holder's side:
///
///   1. the line fires; the KERNEL masks it and marks this object ready;
///   2. a `Waiter.wait` answers with this attachment's key and the line;
///   3. the driver touches its device — everything that reads or writes a
///      register belongs HERE, before the ack, which is §9's release rule;
///   4. `ack` unmasks, and a device still asserting fires again immediately.
///
/// AT MOST ONE UNACKED FIRE EXISTS, ever, because step 1 masks. That is what
/// makes a single-task driver non-reentrant with no lock: the line cannot come
/// back while the handler runs.
///
/// Built by `Process.interrupt_bind` and by nothing else — no word
/// constructor, no word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
public struct Interrupt {
    handle: InterruptHandle,
}

public extension Interrupt {
    /// End this fire and let the device interrupt again (spec §9's ack).
    /// Requires `InterruptRight.Ack`.
    ///
    /// ACK IS A RELEASE: everything that touches device registers happens
    /// BEFORE it, and code after it runs on data already extracted. A driver
    /// that needs register work after the ack serializes it itself; the kernel
    /// does not enforce single-servicing.
    ///
    /// Acking an Interrupt that has not fired TERMINATES the process. It is
    /// caller-checkable in the strongest sense — the only way to learn about a
    /// fire is to have been told about one — and tolerating it would mean the
    /// NEXT fire got acked by a driver that never serviced it.
    public func ack(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_interrupt_ack(self.handle))
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Clock and Timer (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle to a Clock — a granted time source.
///
/// It answers two questions and holds no state: what time is it, and give me
/// something that fires at a time. There is no way to SET one — a monotonic
/// clock that could be set would not be monotonic.
///
/// Built by `System.clock_get` and by nothing else — no word constructor, no
/// word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
public struct Clock {
    handle: ClockHandle,
}

public extension Clock {
    /// Nanoseconds on this clock. Requires `ClockRight.Read`.
    ///
    /// THE EPOCH IS NOT PUBLISHED and must not be relied on: `Monotonic` counts
    /// from whenever the machine started counting, so two readings are
    /// comparable and one reading means nothing on its own. What it guarantees
    /// is that it never steps and never goes backwards, which is what a
    /// duration measurement and a timeout both actually need.
    ///
    /// THE BUFFER IS THIS FRAME'S and a caller never sees it, exactly as
    /// `Waiter.wait`'s is: the kernel answers 64 bits through a copy-out record
    /// because one profile's registers are 32 wide, and supplying the bytes
    /// here is what keeps a raw address out of the typed surface.
    public func now(&self) -> Result<UInt64, SosStatus> {
        now_into_frame(self.handle)
    }

    /// Make a Timer that measures against THIS clock, for its whole life.
    /// Requires `ClockRight.TimerCreate`.
    ///
    /// It comes back DISARMED and owing nothing: a Timer is a schedule you have
    /// not written yet, so attaching one to a Waiter before arming it parks
    /// rather than reporting. A full timer slab comes back as
    /// `SosStatus.NoResource`, which the caller could not have known.
    public func timer_create(&self) -> Result<Timer, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, ClockOp.TimerCreate as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
        return Timer(handle: TimerHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `Clock.now`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). The buffer's address never leaves it.
func now_into_frame(handle: ClockHandle) unsafe -> Result<UInt64, SosStatus> {
    var buf: [UInt64; CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS] = [0; CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS]
    try checked(sos_clock_now(handle, (&var buf) as UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                              clock_now_record_bytes() as UInt))
    return buf[CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT]
}

/// A handle to a Timer — a deadline on the Clock that made it.
///
/// THE PROCESS-SLEEP PRIMITIVE, and the thing that makes waiting on time
/// possible at all: attach one to a Waiter, arm it, and the wait returns when it
/// fires. Before this object a process could block forever or not at all.
///
/// SPEC §2'S CYCLE, from the holder's side:
///
///   1. `arm(after_ns:)` — one-shot — or `arm(after_ns:interval_ns:)` for a
///      repeating schedule;
///   2. a `Waiter.wait` answers with this attachment's key and a FIRE COUNT;
///   3. there is NO ACK. A periodic timer has already re-armed itself, and the
///      wait that reported the fires is what consumed them.
///
/// THE COUNT IS WHY STEP 3 IS SAFE. A consumer descheduled across several
/// periods is told HOW MANY expired rather than being handed them one at a time
/// or losing them — so a slow consumer degrades into coarser reporting instead
/// of falling permanently behind. The re-arm is DRIFT-FREE: each deadline is
/// measured from the previous DEADLINE, not from when the kernel got round to
/// it, so a timer armed at one millisecond is still on the millisecond a
/// thousand fires later.
///
/// Built by `Clock.timer_create` and by nothing else — no word constructor, no
/// word extractor, the module docstring's rule.
public struct Timer {
    handle: TimerHandle,
}

public extension Timer {
    /// Fire once `after_ns` nanoseconds from now, and then every `interval_ns`
    /// after that. Requires `TimerRight.Arm`.
    ///
    /// `interval_ns` DEFAULTS TO ZERO, WHICH IS A ONE-SHOT — the timer fires
    /// once and disarms itself. That is the default because a timeout is the
    /// common case and a repeating schedule is the deliberate one.
    ///
    /// `after_ns` is RELATIVE to now, not an absolute time, so a caller never
    /// has to read the clock first and race between two syscalls.
    ///
    /// **ARMING AN ARMED TIMER REPLACES ITS SCHEDULE** and discards any fires it
    /// had not yet delivered — it is how a timeout is CHANGED, not an error. A
    /// caller who wants the outstanding fires reads them with a wait first.
    ///
    /// The arguments cross as a COPY-IN RECORD out of this frame, because two
    /// 64-bit times are four words on a 32-bit profile and an op has three
    /// argument registers. A caller never sees the buffer, for the reason
    /// `Waiter.wait` gives about its own.
    ///
    /// A schedule finer than the machine can serve is not refused: fires
    /// COALESCE into the count, so an interval below the kernel's resolution
    /// reports many fires per wake rather than wedging the machine.
    public func arm(&self, after_ns: UInt64,
                    interval_ns: UInt64 = 0) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        arm_from_frame(self.handle, after_ns, interval_ns)
    }

    /// Stop this timer and discard any fires it had not delivered. Requires
    /// `TimerRight.Arm`.
    ///
    /// **DISARMING ONE THAT IS NOT ARMED IS A NO-OP**, and that is the deliberate
    /// opposite of `Interrupt.ack`, which faults when nothing has fired. The
    /// difference is whether the caller could have known: an extra ack is
    /// provably a bug, since the only way to learn about a fire is to be told
    /// about one — while a ONE-SHOT DISARMS ITSELF, so whether a timer is still
    /// armed is kernel state that changed without the caller acting. Cancelling
    /// a timeout that has just expired is the ordinary race in every
    /// select-with-timeout, and faulting it would make the safe idiom
    /// unwritable.
    public func disarm(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_timer_disarm(self.handle))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `Timer.arm`, confined to one small named helper
/// (design 136). The record's address never leaves it.
func arm_from_frame(handle: TimerHandle, after_ns: UInt64,
                    interval_ns: UInt64) unsafe -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    var buf: [UInt64; TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS] = [0; TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS]
    buf[TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT] = after_ns
    buf[TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT] = interval_ns
    checked(sos_timer_arm(handle, (&var buf) as UnsafePointer<UInt64>,
                          timer_arm_record_bytes() as UInt))
}

// =============================================================================
// Waiter (spec §2.2; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// Decode a record the kernel copied out into `WaitResult`.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG PANICS, loudly, and that is deliberate. Every other decode in
/// this module treats its input as data a process might legitimately be handed;
/// this one does not, because the KERNEL wrote it out of the same `WaitTag`
/// declaration this function reads. A tag that does not decode is therefore a
/// skew between the kernel and the `sos` module it exports — a bug in the pair,
/// not a runtime condition — and the never-hide rule says a bug the caller
/// cannot act on should stop the program where it happened rather than become
/// an `Err` somebody maps to "unknown".
func decode_wait(key: UInt, tag: UInt, payload: UInt) -> WaitResult {
    guard let kind = WaitTag.from(raw: tag) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote wait tag {}, which this build cannot name", tag)
    }
    match kind {
        case Event -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Event(word: payload)),
        case Interrupt -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                     what: WaitPayload.Interrupt(line: payload)),
        case Timer -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                 what: WaitPayload.Timer(fires: payload)),
    }
}

/// A handle to a Waiter — §2.2's generic wait aggregator, epoll/Port-style.
///
/// Attachments are PERSISTENT subscriptions: `add` once, and the handle stays
/// watched until `remove`. There is no one-shot mode, and readiness is
/// LEVEL-TRIGGERED, so a signal that lands before anyone waits is still there
/// when the wait arrives.
///
/// Several threads may park on one Waiter (§2.2), and a ready handle wakes one
/// of them; a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and attaching it twice
/// terminates the process, on the faults ruling's terms — it is a mistake only
/// the attacher could have made.
public struct Waiter {
    handle: WaiterHandle,
}

public extension Waiter {
    /// Attach `event` under `key`, which `wait` hands back when it becomes
    /// ready. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and `EventRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// KEYS ARE UNIQUE PER WAITER: `key` must not already name an attachment on
    /// this Waiter, and reusing one TERMINATES the process. It is the invariant
    /// `remove` and the wait answer both rest on — with two attachments under
    /// one key, neither could say which it meant.
    ///
    /// IT TAKES A `&Event` AND NOT A HANDLE WORD. That is the module
    /// docstring's rule reaching cross-type plumbing: there is no way to spell
    /// a handle you were not given, so an attachment names an object this
    /// process holds by construction. The field access below is same-module,
    /// which is why no accessor is owed and none exists.
    ///
    /// ONE METHOD PER WAITABLE KIND, and it stays that way on purpose — see the
    /// brief's note on why a `Waitable` trait is not the shape (a public trait
    /// with a handle-producing requirement is a forged-handle door under the
    /// orphan rule, and Saw has no sealed traits yet). Kinds are a LIST, so
    /// overloads are a list; it is MODES that multiply, which is why
    /// `event_create` takes its mode as a value.
    ///
    /// The second kind is here now, and it is what the shape predicted: each
    /// overload is TWO LINES delegating to one internal, so the list grows by a
    /// method per kind and the wire call has one caller.
    public func add(&self, event: &Event, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(event.handle, key)
    }

    /// Attach an Interrupt under `key`, which `wait` hands back with the line
    /// when it fires. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and
    /// `InterruptRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// Every rule the Event overload states holds unchanged: keys are unique
    /// per Waiter, a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and readiness is
    /// level-triggered — so an Interrupt that fired BEFORE it was attached is
    /// reported by this call, which is the ordering a driver hits when the
    /// device was already asserting at bring-up.
    public func add(&self, interrupt: &Interrupt, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(interrupt.handle, key)
    }

    /// Attach a Timer under `key`, which `wait` hands back with the FIRE COUNT
    /// when it comes due. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and
    /// `TimerRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// The third kind, and every rule the other two state holds unchanged: keys
    /// are unique per Waiter, a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and
    /// readiness is level-triggered — so a Timer that fired BEFORE it was
    /// attached is reported by this call rather than lost.
    ///
    /// A DISARMED Timer is simply never ready, so attaching one and waiting
    /// parks until somebody arms it. That is what makes "arm, then wait" and
    /// "attach, then arm" both work.
    public func add(&self, timer: &Timer, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(timer.handle, key)
    }

    /// The one call the per-kind overloads share. Private: a raw handle word
    /// crosses here and nowhere else in this type, which is the module
    /// docstring's rule spelled as a boundary rather than repeated per method.
    func attach(&self, target: UInt, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_waiter_add(self.handle, target, key))
    }

    /// Stop watching whatever is attached under `key`. A key this Waiter does
    /// not use terminates the process. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach`.
    ///
    /// THE KEY IS WHAT NAMES AN ATTACHMENT, not the waitable: detaching edits
    /// this Waiter's own table and never touches the object on the other end,
    /// so it needs no handle and no right over it. That is also what will let a
    /// stale attachment be removed once handles can be closed — at which point
    /// there is no handle left to name it with.
    public func remove(&self, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_waiter_remove(self.handle, key))
    }

    /// Block until an attached handle is ready, and answer with the attacher's
    /// key and what fired. Requires `WaiterRight.Wait`.
    ///
    /// Returns IMMEDIATELY when something is already ready, which is what
    /// level-triggering buys: the readiness a signal left behind is still
    /// there, so "signal then wait" and "wait then signal" have the same
    /// outcome and only the second one parks.
    ///
    /// THE BUFFER IS THIS FRAME'S, and a caller never sees it. The kernel
    /// answers a wait by copying a record into the caller's own memory (it has
    /// no allocator and nowhere to put one that would outlive the call), so
    /// somebody has to supply the bytes; supplying them HERE is what keeps a
    /// raw address out of the typed surface, exactly as the handle rule keeps
    /// raw handle words out. The stack is the right place for them: the record
    /// is three words and the frame outlives the park, since the frame is the
    /// parked thread's own stack.
    public func wait(&self) -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
        wait_into_frame(self.handle)
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `wait`, confined to one small named helper (design 136).
/// The buffer's address never leaves it.
func wait_into_frame(handle: WaiterHandle) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
    var buf: [UInt; WAIT_RECORD_WORDS] = [0; WAIT_RECORD_WORDS]
    try checked(sos_waiter_wait(handle, (&var buf) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                wait_record_bytes() as UInt))
    return decode_wait(buf[WAIT_KEY_WORD], buf[WAIT_TAG_WORD],
                       buf[WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD])
}

// =============================================================================
// The runtime's two hooks, process side (design 172 part 2)
// =============================================================================
//
// `sosrt` implements the frozen `__saw_rt_*` seams over exactly two per-side
// hooks — where a byte goes, and how the machine stops. The kernel HAL answers
// "the UART" and "the board's stop". A PROCESS owns no device, so it answers
// "a System op" to both, and that is the M/U split being real rather than
// claimed: if `panic()` could reach a UART from userspace, it would not be.
//
// WHY THEY LIVE HERE and not in `sos/hal/<arch>/user/`, where they were C until
// now. They name no architecture — a byte reaches the console through a System
// op, which is the same op on both profiles — so two per-arch copies were two
// copies of one thing. They belong to whoever owns the System object, which is
// this module. What kept them C was DF-172e, the `noreturn` panic sink Saw
// could not type; design 177 closed it, and `sos/hal/<arch>/user/syscall.c` is
// now the syscall instruction and nothing else, exactly as its own header said
// it should be.

/// The boot handle, parked for the seams below.
///
/// The runtime seams take no handle — the ABI has no room for one — so the one
/// root is given at boot is remembered here by `System.init` before anything
/// can print. It is root's own authority being kept in root's own address
/// space, NOT ambient authority: a process that was never given the handle has
/// nothing to remember and its panics are simply silent.
///
/// `unsafe static var` (design 149) rather than an `Atomic`: it is plain
/// compound process state written once at boot, and spec §7's uniprocessor
/// argument covers it exactly as it covers the arena.
unsafe static var SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 0

/// Park the boot handle. A small named `unsafe` helper is how design 136 says
/// to confine contact with an unsafe-typed value, and it is what keeps the
/// public `System(boot_handle:)` an ordinary safe signature.
func park_system_handle(handle: UInt) unsafe {
    SYSTEM_HANDLE = handle
}

/// Write `len` bytes at `ptr` to the debug console. THE PANIC PATH'S WRITER.
///
/// CHECK-FREE BY CONSTRUCTION, on the same terms as the kernel HAL's writer
/// (DF-172b) and for the same reason: this runs while a panic is being
/// reported, so a bounds check or an overflow check that tripped here would
/// re-enter `__saw_rt_panic` and either hang or garble the one diagnostic that
/// matters. So no indexing (raw `UnsafeMemory` reads), no allocation, no
/// `String`, and a cursor that advances with WRAPPING arithmetic (`&+` / `&-`)
/// so even a nonsensical length cannot raise an overflow trap.
///
/// A per-byte status is deliberately dropped. There is nowhere to report it TO
/// — the console is the thing that would have failed — and inventing a second
/// channel for "the console is broken" is how a panic path stops being one.
/// The typed surface above is where a caller who CAN act on a failure gets it.
@export("sos_rt_write")
func rt_write(ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, len: UInt) unsafe {
    if SYSTEM_HANDLE == 0 {
        return
    }
    var addr = ptr as UInt
    var left = len
    while left != 0 {
        let byte = UnsafeMemory<UInt8, Normal>(addr).read()
        let _ = sos_system_debug_print(SYSTEM_HANDLE, byte as UInt)
        addr = addr &+ 1
        left = left &- 1
    }
}

/// Stop the machine with `code`, for the common runtime.
///
/// `-> Never` is what `sosrt` declares this hook to be, and it is true twice
/// over: `shutdown` does not return when it succeeds, and when it is refused —
/// no handle was ever parked, or the right was stripped — there is nothing left
/// to try, so the loop is what keeps a failed abort from running off the end of
/// the granted region.
@export("sos_rt_abort")
func rt_abort(code: UInt32) unsafe -> Never {
    let _ = sos_system_shutdown(SYSTEM_HANDLE, code as UInt)
    while { }
}

